#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044} :activity/id #uuid "e1f24137-95a9-5d07-991d-2ce3ba7ada0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230177 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with constant coefficients given by\n\n$$\nay' + by = f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nAs we know, the method of finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x),$ we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it. \n\nAn ODE exhibits **exponential forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = Ke^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a constant. \n\nProvided that the forcing function $e^{kx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, our trial guess for the particular solution is an exponential function with the same exponent. That is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha\\,e^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with **exponential forcing**. Let's look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7a60ff4f-cb9d-522a-a94a-d16f43b6a04a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14449 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0804d262-6611-5c45-bc86-10e3c26e86a2" :step/math-academy-id 597439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230227 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 4 e ^ {2x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 0\n$$\n\nand therefore, the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) =  A e^{-3x},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Let's now find the **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ to this equation.\n\nSince the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is the exponential function $f(x) = 4 e^{2x},$ and this is not incorporated within the complementary solution, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2+3)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p = A e^{-3x} + \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can employ a similar strategy when the forcing function is a sum of exponential functions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "62cac4a5-105b-5e43-8d88-63ce73a86e94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14700 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c3a6d6f6-44d9-519b-8417-7113691d787c" :step/math-academy-id 597440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "bfa02bee-ddb6-54c2-afc0-6e67786db1f4" :step/math-academy-id 597441 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230178 :tutorial/content "So far, our guesses for the particular solution have worked because the forcing term did not already appear in the complementary solution.\n\nSometimes, however, the right-hand side of the differential equation has the same form as a term that appears in the complementary solution $y_c(x).$ When this happens, our usual guess for $y_p(x)$ fails because it duplicates part of the complementary solution and cannot produce a new solution.\n\nIn this situation, we must modify our guess by *multiplying it by $x.$* Specifically, if the forcing term has the form $K e^{kx}$ and the complementary solution contains a term like $Ae^{kx},$ then instead of guessing\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{kx},\n$$\n\nwe try\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\boldsymbol{x} e^{kx}.\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying by $x$ creates a new function that is *linearly independent* of the complementary solution. As a result, our guess no longer overlaps with $y_c(x)$ and can serve as a valid particular solution. We'll discuss linear independence of functions in more detail in future lessons.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice using a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e6655774-c9ab-58c6-bfec-5013b08b7918" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14450 :tutorial/title "Cases When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "b7dc2094-d676-591f-98e7-87919cdf8889" :step/math-academy-id 597445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230229 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 10e^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 0\n$$\n\nand so the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNotice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 10 e^{-5x},$ and yet $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution. So, in this case, we assume a particular solution that takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x})+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 10x  e ^ {-5x}\n$$\n\nand thus the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-5x} + 10xe^{-5x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5cb7b6d-2997-5082-a597-8a0e6e4eaf5b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14703 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "11a3780c-c51f-5b21-bfc3-9b6034b2e639" :step/math-academy-id 597446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483350}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "3bc4e70a-50ff-55b4-930f-052f89424e30" :step/math-academy-id 597447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308403}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "5976dd4e-9c4c-50fb-83c5-3e9bcb60cd32" :step/math-academy-id 597451 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531856} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531853}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "fc2e409a-3ef9-58dc-81db-f72435bd3a6c" :step/math-academy-id 597454}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229250}]}
